course: dictionary
lesson: 9566
title: Straits: narrow passages of sea
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A strait is a narrow passage of sea between two pieces of land. It joins two larger seas, so ships can sail through it instead of going the long way round.
explain: «Most goods now cross the sea in standard steel boxes on giant ships, squeezing through a few narrow passages called chokepoints, like the Strait of Malacca.» The words narrow passages tell you that a strait is a narrow passage, and the word the Strait of Malacca is a name you can find on a map.
explain: Strait also has a second meaning. «In dire straits» means in a very difficult or dangerous situation, often with money. Here the word has nothing to do with water, so always check the words around it.
fact: A strait is a narrow passage of sea between two pieces of land.
fact: A strait joins two larger seas, and ships use straits as shortcuts.
fact: The Strait of Gibraltar lies between Europe and Africa.
fact: The Bering Strait lies between Asia and North America.
fact: «In dire straits» means in a very difficult or dangerous situation.
exercise: «Most goods now cross the sea in standard steel boxes on giant ships, squeezing through a few narrow passages called chokepoints, like the Strait of Malacca.» What is a strait? | A large lake inside a country | A tall mountain range | A kind of giant steel ship | *A narrow passage of sea between two pieces of land
exercise: «Today straits still matter: about a fifth of the oil the world uses normally passes through the Strait of Hormuz, so trouble there can make petrol dearer far away.» Why would trouble in the Strait of Hormuz make petrol dearer far away? | Because petrol is made in the strait | Because the strait is a large lake | Because ships never use straits | *Because a lot of oil must pass through this narrow sea passage
exercise: Which two continents does the Strait of Gibraltar lie between? | New Zealand and Antarctica | Asia and North America | *Europe and Africa | Australia and Tasmania
test: Ayesha reads, «He was in dire straits after the flood ruined his shop.» What does dire straits mean here? | A sea with no land around it | *In a very difficult or dangerous situation | Sailing very fast through a narrow sea | Having lots of money to spend
test: «The Baltic Sea is brackish, much less salty than the ocean, because many rivers pour fresh water into it and its only exit is through narrow Danish straits.» Why is the Baltic Sea less salty than the ocean? | *Many rivers pour fresh water into it | The Danish straits make it saltier | It has no rivers at all | Its water is always frozen
exam: «Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, Māori and Native American peoples are the first peoples of their lands, and their cultures deserve respect.» Which statement about these peoples is true? | They arrived in their lands after everyone else | *They are the first peoples of their lands | They live only in the Baltic Sea | Their cultures do not matter
